Your Docker build is slow. How do you optimize layer caching?


    Docker builds images layer by layer. Each Dockerfile instruction (FROM, RUN, COPY, ADD, etc.) creates a new layer. Docker reuses cached layers if the instruction and its inputs haven't changed. To optimize builds, I structure the Dockerfile so that stable layers, like dependency installation, come before frequently changing application code. I also use .dockerignore, multi-stage builds, BuildKit, and persistent cache in CI/CD to avoid rebuilding unchanged layers.

    2. Correct Layer Ordering

        Instead, copy dependency files first:

        FROM python:3.12

        WORKDIR /app

        COPY requirements.txt .

        RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

        COPY . .

        CMD ["python", "app.py"]

    First Build

        Layer 1 ✓ Base image
        Layer 2 ✓ requirements.txt
        Layer 3 ✓ pip install
        Layer 4 ✓ Copy source


    Second Build (only app.py changed)

        Layer 1 ✓ Cache
        Layer 2 ✓ Cache
        Layer 3 ✓ Cache
        Layer 4 Rebuild only

    Dependency installation is skipped.


  Optimization: 

    Better Example
      COPY pom.xml .

      RUN mvn dependency:go-offline

      COPY src ./src

      RUN mvn clean package -DskipTests

    Use .dockerignore

        A large build context slows builds.

        Bad:

        COPY . .

        includes:

        .git/
        logs/
        venv/
        node_modules/
        __pycache__/
        .idea/
        dist/

        Use a .dockerignore file:

        .git
        node_modules
        venv
        dist
        __pycache__
        *.log
        .env
        coverage

    Combine RUN Commands

    Bad:

      RUN apt update

      RUN apt install -y curl

      RUN apt clean

      Creates three layers.

    Better:

      RUN apt-get update && \
          apt-get install -y curl && \
          rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*


    Use Multi-Stage Builds


    Enable Docker BuildKit

    BuildKit provides:

        Parallel execution
        Improved caching
        Cache mounts
        Secret mounts
        Inline cache export

    DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1 docker build .

    RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache/pip \
    pip install -r requirements.txt




How do you pass build-time vs run-time variables?


  docker build \
    --build-arg APP_VERSION=1.5.2 \
    -t myapp .

  docker run myapp env

  Override Runtime Variables
  
  docker run \
      -e LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG \
      myapp

  ARG is used for build-time configuration and exists only during the image build. It's suitable for values like dependency versions, build profiles, or base image tags. ENV is used for runtime configuration and is available to the application when the container starts. Runtime values can be overridden with docker run -e, Docker Compose environment variables, or Kubernetes ConfigMaps and Secrets. I never store secrets in ARG or ENV; instead, I use a dedicated secret management solution such as Kubernetes Secrets, Vault, or a cloud secrets manager. This keeps the image immutable, secure, and portable.


